.TH XDP-BFD-OBSERVE 8 "2026-09-20" "xdp-bfd" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
xdp-bfd-observe \- attach the BFD XDP program and watch what it sees
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xdp-bfd-observe
.I interface
.RB [ --generic ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Attaches the same XDP program
.BR xdp-bfd (8)
uses to
.IR interface ,
prints the session table once a second, and logs liveness events as they
arrive on the ring buffer. It is a debugging tool: it observes, it never
answers a packet, and it runs no control plane.
.PP
It attaches the program in
.B promiscuous
mode, which is the difference that matters. In that mode the program
stops dropping control packets for address pairs no session covers and
passes them to the stack instead, so unconfigured traffic can be watched.
That is also why it
.B must not
be run on a production interface: it reopens the flood path to the
socket that the engine's unknown-session drop exists to close.
.PP
Attaching replaces whatever XDP program the interface already has, so
running this on an interface an engine is serving takes that engine's
fast path away.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --generic
Attach in generic (skb) mode rather than native. Slower, and works on
drivers or virtual interfaces with no native XDP support.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I observer.csv
Session table samples, appended, in the working directory.
.TP
.I events.csv
Liveness events, appended, in the working directory.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/xdp-bfd/bfd_xdp.o
The XDP object, found with no argument.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Non-zero if the object cannot be loaded or the attach fails. Otherwise it
runs until interrupted.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xdp-bfd (8)
